barry cullen sales manager for a duck  farm is making his daily rounds as a  native of Northern Ireland he's feeling  a bit concerned these days he's worried  that if Britain were to leave the EU it  could have negative consequences for the  farm which lies right on the border  between Northern Ireland and the  Republic of Ireland the farm might end  up having to pay customs on meat exports  to the north should Britain leave the EU  the border to the single market would  run right through County Monaghan the  road signs change from miles per hour to  kilometers per hour so that's how close  we are to that the border the border  also symbolizes an age-old conflict  marking the edge of the United Kingdom  for Protestants and dividing an island  that many Catholics see as one nation  the EU has been a decisive factor in  keeping the peace on both sides of the  border for two decades now you could set  the the political landscape in Northern  Ireland bike you know 15-20 years to  what it was like whenever the the  British Army was in the north and when  you were travelling anywhere you would  always have to be stopped your car you'd  have to get out you know  searches we were always searched and so  a lot of it wasn't a very nice time huge  police stations like this one in the  border village of cross Moglen a  Catholic stronghold in Northern Ireland  or former customs posts are relics of  that era before the peace process the  inner Irish border was among the most  closely monitored in Europe says Anthony  Suarez so a lorry driver passing from  the Republic of Ireland coming into  Northern Ireland  at this building the lorry driver would  have have to enter this building with  his all the documentation declaring what  he had on board his lorry later the  British authorities converted the  customs post to checkpoints in the 1970s  and 80s many of the smaller crossing  points were demolished and the border  fortified British forces kept an eye out  for terrorists at the official border  crossings Irish farmer Hugh Conway would  not like to see that situation returned  he sees today's open border as a huge  step forward he even remembers the  frequent smuggling that went on here  during World War two the police wouldn't  watch you for small things let you go  small things when you had nothing it  used to be difficult for the farmers in  the south to do business with the north  Hugh Conway's farm was right behind a  closed border crossing we couldn't take  cattle just through that room you had a  second way back to the safe and these  priests had extended cattle we're with  our Maher and Killian the Northern Irish  farmers wouldn't like to see a return to  the old days either Keith and Alan  Wilson are cattle breeders Protestants  and optimists the elder Wilson can still  remember the customs duties but they  hope that any reintroduction of border  checks would have only economic and not  political consequences for Northern  Ireland if it didn't happen to me that  they would leave it I don't think it'll  affect the peace process I think it's  going far enough now for - for the sound  of his own fate Northern Ireland's  largest Protestant party backs a brexit  and says farmers shouldn't worry about  leaving the EU most Catholics on the  other hand like berry oppose leaving the  EU  Barry Cullen may enjoy a tight horse  race but not close political ones he's  worried that the great majority of  voters in Britain simply don't  understand what's actually at stake if I  was betting I would say that they'll  stay in but a very low odds bet I think  even 50/50 Barry is voting YES in the  referendum and he hopes that in future  he will still be able to enjoy his quick  trips across the border to bet on the  horses with the Pope
 

